meta {
  name: 1 Availability (any authenticated caller)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}
get {
  url: {{gatewayUrl}}/api/v1/calendar/availability/{{providerEmail}}?days=90&service=
}
params:query {
  days: 90
  service: 
}
tests {
  test("200 even for a caller who is not the provider", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
  test("free start times only — never appointment detail", function () {
    const body = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(body).to.not.include("emailCustomer");
    expect(body).to.not.include("appointmentStatus");
  });
  test("honours the requested window", function () {
    const days = new Set(res.getBody().data.map(s => s.substring(0, 10)));
    expect(days.size).to.be.at.most(90);
  });
}
docs {
  **Ownership guard removed 2026-08-29, deliberately.** A customer has to see a provider's free slots to
  book one, and `AssertOwner` made this route answer `403` to every customer — the booking flow was
  impossible. Authentication is still required.

  What that exposes is bounded by the response shape: **free start times and nothing else** — no
  appointment, no counterparty, no service, no reason. Busy time is only inferable as "absent from this
  list", which is inherent to any booking product. `2 Appointments` stays owner-only, because that one
  *does* carry customer emails.

  - `days` — window length, clamped to 1..90 server-side (`AvailabilityCalculator.MaxDays`).
  - `service` — a service NAME belonging to this provider. Slots are sized to that service's
    `durationMinutes`, so a 90-minute service is never offered a start time that would run into the next
    appointment. Unknown/blank falls back to a 60-minute grid rather than returning nothing.

  An empty list is now `200`, not `404`: a fully-booked provider is not "not found". `404` means only
  that `{email}` matches no provider.
}
